64-bit counterpart of ../tiger/: the same fork-tiger test programs, but
compiled with fork-tiger's tigerc -64, which emits bits64 C-- (target ...
wordsize 64 pointersize 64) instead of the default bits32. Generated with
regenerate-c--.sh; see that script's header for how to refresh a file.

Expected stdout/exit codes are the same Tiger programs computing the same
results, so input/ and output/ here are symlinks to ../tiger/'s, not
copies - both hold word-size-independent stdin/expected-stdout data, not
generated code, so there's nothing to diverge.

tigermain-riscv64.o/stdlib-riscv64.a are checked in exactly like ../tiger/'s
per-backend artefacts, regenerated by hand with regenerate-riscv64.sh (needs
a fork-tiger checkout; run-tiger64-riscv64.sh itself does not, but does need
the riscv64 cross toolchain - see that script's own comment). There is no
tigermain-alpha.o/stdlib-alpha.a or run-tiger64-alpha.sh yet - -alpha's own
instruction selection has gaps below (regardless of runtime correctness),
so there is nothing to link against yet.

Status (2026-08-17): all 14 .c-- files elaborate cleanly (`qc -driver_elab`)
against every backend's metrics, i.e. they are well-formed bits64 C--.
`make test-tiger64-riscv64` (tests/run-tiger64-riscv64.sh) reaches 13/15
against tests/expected/tiger64-riscv64.txt - colmajor and merge remain (see
"Known failures" below). -alpha does not codegen at all yet:
  - alloc_one: no registers to spill (even on hello) - alpha's
    register allocator/spiller can't handle these frames
  - "No acceptable widths for %mul/%quot on target 'alpha'; asked for
    64->64->64" - alpha's instruction selection only has 32-bit mul/quot

Getting from 0/15 to 13/15 on riscv64 needed fixes in three different
places, each documented with a "claude:" comment at its own site:

1. fork-tiger's tigerc -64 output itself was invalid C-- under a bits64
   target (frontend/frame.ml, backend/codegen.ml in fork-tiger): the GC
   descriptor tables' bare integer literals defaulted to the target's
   native word size (64) instead of their declared bits32, and the
   boolean-widening-after-a-comparison was hardcoded to %sx32 regardless
   of word size.

2. fork-tiger's hand-written runtime (runtime/gc.c, stdlib/stdlib.c,
   stdlib/stdlib.h in fork-tiger) hardcoded "unsigned" (always 32 bits)
   for heap pointers and the allocation header - this is what "fork-tiger's
   runtime hardcoding bits32 pointers" meant in earlier notes. Ported to
   uintptr_t, a no-op on every existing 32-bit backend (confirmed: every
   other backend's own regenerate-*.sh + run-tiger-*.sh still matches its
   recorded baseline after this). The mechanical counterpart on the .c--
   side (bits32 -> bits64, "+4" -> "+8", alignment mask widened) lives in
   this directory's regenerate-riscv64.sh, applied at generation time
   rather than checked into fork-tiger, mirroring how ../tiger/'s own
   regenerate-ppc.sh does its byteorder flip outside fork-tiger.

3. qc-- itself (this repo) had two more bugs, neither specific to
   fork-tiger, both found chasing this suite down from 0/15:
     - arch/riscv64/riscv64.ml + riscv64rec.mlb had no %quot rule at all
       (riscv32's sibling does - RV64M's "div" ported straight across,
       operating on full 64-bit registers instead of RV32M's 32-bit
       ones) - colmajor/merge/sieve need real integer division.
     - runtime/qc--runtime.h's Cmm_Word - the type backing pc_map_entry's
       location-encoded fields (register/stack-slot locations, the
       return-address location, span values, register/local/span
       counts) AND every register/stack-pointer value the GC's stack
       walk reads through it - was hardcoded "unsigned" (32 bits). qc--
       itself emits every one of those fields at the target's native
       pointer width (confirmed from a -riscv64 .s dump: inalloc/
       outalloc/return_addressp/num_registers/etc. are all ".dword", and
       MKOFFSET(0) shows up as bit 63 set, not bit 31) - so on a 64-bit
       target the C runtime was reading these fields at the wrong
       stride/width, corrupting the very first activation-record walk a
       real GC cycle ever performed under a 64-bit backend (every
       existing backend is 32-bit, so this was never exercised before).
       Retyped Cmm_Word itself to uintptr_t (qc--runtime.h) and widened
       pcmap.c's Cmm_loctype/Cmm_as_register/Cmm_as_offset decoding to
       match (their GOODBITS already derives from sizeof() of their own
       local variable, so this generalized for free once that variable's
       type did). This is the single fix that took the suite from
       crashing on every GC-triggering test (arrays, queens, qsort,
       sieve, wf, wff, rb, rc4 - anything allocating enough to ever
       collect) to passing on all of them. uintptr_t equals plain
       "unsigned" on every 32-bit backend, so this is a no-op there too -
       confirmed the same way as fix 2 (every other run-tiger-*.sh still
       matches its baseline).
   This is real, general qc-- backend work, not tiger64-specific - it is
   very likely also why -alpha's tiger tests were "blocked" (see the
   fork's own earlier notes), on top of that backend's separate
   instruction-selection gaps above.

Known failures (both confirmed independent of heap size, i.e. not a
GC-timing flake - see the dated investigation notes in tests/tiger64/ git
history for the full trace if this needs picking back up):
  - colmajor: "malloc(): corrupted top size" - crashes with no output at
    the default 8192-byte heap, but runs correctly to completion
    (matching output exactly) with TIGER_HEAPSIZE=1000000 (env var
    runtime/runtime.c-- reads). So this is real heap corruption, but only
    once the copying collector actually runs, and only for colmajor's
    specific allocation pattern (2D arrays) - wf/rb/rc4/sieve all
    allocate plenty and collect plenty without tripping it. Root cause
    not yet found.
  - merge: exits 0 instead of the expected 1, but stdout matches exactly
    (byte for byte) at any heap size, including the huge one above - so
    this is not a GC bug. merge.tig's whole body is
    "printlist(merge(list1,list2))", whose value (and so the program's
    exit code, via runtime.c--'s "rv = tiger_main(0)") is whatever the
    base case's "print(\"\n\")" returns (1, tig_print's printf-return-
    count convention - same as hello's rc=14) - propagated back up
    through several levels of recursive printlist calls. Something in
    that chain drops the return value between there and main on riscv64;
    a calling-convention/tail-call return-value bug is the leading
    suspect, not yet confirmed.
